---
title: REACHED
summary: Researching Equity and Access in Community-based HEalthcare for Diagnostics
peopleSummary: Examines whether community diagnostic centres provide equitable access to diagnostic services.
highlights:
  - Comparing access to community diagnostic centres with hospital diagnostic services across selected sites in the North East and North Cumbria.
  - Analysing de-identified appointment data to examine whether access and attendance differ between groups.
  - Interviewing patients and staff to understand experiences, choices and barriers to diagnostic testing.
researchThemes:
  - community-diagnostics
  - healthcare-access
whyItMatters:
  heading: Why this study matters
  body: >-
    Community Diagnostic Centres (CDCs) are places where people can have healthcare tests, such as scans, away from a hospital. These tests help doctors and nurses understand what may be happening inside a person’s body and whether treatment is needed.


    CDCs aim to make diagnostic tests easier to access. However, we do not yet know whether they work equally well for everyone. REACHED will examine the Metrocentre and Workington CDCs and compare access with hospital testing services in Newcastle, Gateshead and North Cumbria.
approach:
  heading: What we are doing
  body: >-
    The study has two parts. First, the research team will analyse appointment information already collected by the CDCs and hospitals. This will include patients’ age group, sex, ethnic group, level of disadvantage based on where they live, broad location, type of healthcare test, referring hospital department, intended testing location and whether the appointment was attended. Researchers will not receive patient names, addresses, full postcodes, NHS numbers or contact details.


    Second, the team will interview approximately 21–33 CDC staff and 24–34 adults who have received healthcare tests at CDCs and local hospitals. Interviews will last approximately 40–60 minutes, take place face to face or through Microsoft Teams, and be audio-recorded. Each participant will receive a £25 voucher as a thank-you for their time.


    Evidence from both parts will be considered together to understand how easily people can obtain tests, the choices available to them and whether they attend appointments.
impact:
  heading: Impact and outputs
  body: >-
    REACHED is designed to produce evidence about whether Community Diagnostic Centres improve access to testing equitably. The findings may help NHS organisations understand where barriers remain and inform changes intended to make diagnostic services easier to access for different groups of people.
status: ongoing
currentStage: study-setup
lead: Dr Amber Sacre
teamMembers:
